Title: Mail Fraud Standard - Otherly (Straightforwardly)}

Article 8. Maryland

1. Definition of Mail Fraud
a. Mail fraud is a criminal offense that involves the use of the U.S. Postal Service or any other means of communication to defraud another person, with the intent to deprive them of their property or rights.
b. The elements of mail fraud include:
i. A scheme or artifice to defraud;
ii. Use of the U.S. Postal Service or any other means of communication in furtherance of the scheme; and
iii. Intent to deprive the victim of money or property.
2. Examples of Mail Fraud
a. False representation of identity or false statements made by a person using the U.S. Postal Service or any other means of communication to obtain goods or services from another person;
b. Misrepresentation of a debt owed by a person to another person through the use of the U.S. Postal Service or any other means of communication;
c. Unauthorized access to a financial account or other personal information through the use of the U.S. Postal Service or any other means of communication;
d. Forgery or alteration of a check or other financial instrument using the U.S. Postal Service or any other means of communication;
e. Impersonating an official or representative of a government agency or private entity to obtain goods or services from another person using the U.S. Postal Service or any other means of communication;
f. Solicitation for funds or property under false pretenses using the U.S. Postal Service or any other means of communication;
g. Concealing or misrepresenting material facts related to a transaction involving the U.S. Postal Service or any other means of communication.
3. Penalties for Mail Fraud
a. Mail fraud is a federal crime punishable by up to 20 years in prison and a fine of up to $250,000.
b. In addition to incarceration and fines, individuals convicted of mail fraud may also be required to pay restitution to the victims of their crimes.
